===Etymology===
From {{suffix|dzīvs|nieks|gloss1=alive|lang=lv}}, or perhaps {{suffix|dzīvot|nieks|gloss1=to live|alt1=dzīv(ot)|lang=lv}}. The original meaning of this word was “living person,” “inhabitant” (now {{term|dzīvotājs|lang=lv}}, {{term|iedzīvotājs}}). The modern meaning of “animal” or “living being” first appeared in the 1860s, in writings by [[w:Juris Alunāns|J. Alunāns]], following the model of {{etyl|ru|-}} {{term|животное|tr=živótnoe|lang=ru}}, and was registered in dictionaries in the 1870s. Formerly other expressions had been used for this meaning (e.g. {{term|dzīvs|lang=lv}} {{term|radījums|lang=lv}} “living creature,” or also more specific terms like {{term|lops|lang=lv}} “farm animal” or {{term|zvērs|lang=lv}} “beast”).<ref>{{R:lv:LEV}}</ref>

